<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pune</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Poona</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pune, formerly Poona, is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan plateau</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> spent his youth there, and in the 18th century it became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> chief ministers, with their palace of Shaniwar Wada. The British took it in 1817 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-maratha-war">Third Anglo-Maratha War</a>. It later became a centre of education and social reform.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pune.jpg" alt="Pune" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ujjawal.Gayakwad · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pune_West_skyline_-_March_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 18th century real power passed from the Chhatrapati at Satara to the Peshwa's administration at <b>Pune</b>, which supervised a confederacy of semi-autonomous chiefs, among them the Gaekwads of Baroda, Holkars of Indore, Scindias of Gwalior and Bhonsles of Nagpur, who collected revenue and maintained armies while…</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Regional bodies such as the Indian Association of Calcutta, founded by Surendranath Banerjea in 1876, the <b>Poona</b> Sarvajanik Sabha and the Madras Mahajana Sabha prepared the ground for a national organisation.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1919 and 1966 scholars at the Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute, <b>Pune</b>, compared manuscripts from India and abroad and produced the Critical Edition, on 13,000 pages in 19 volumes, with the Harivamsha in two more volumes and six index volumes; it is the text usually used in Mahabharata…</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Mahatma Gandhi is said to have sung it over a hundred times while imprisoned at Yerawada Jail in <b>Pune</b> in the 1930s.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sare-jahan-se-accha">Sare Jahan se Accha</a></li><li><blockquote>Bhor merged into Bombay Province in 1948 and became part of Maharashtra in 1960, in <b>Pune</b> district.</blockquote><a href="/state/bhor-state">Bhor State</a></li></ul></section>
